    content_en:
      "Have you ever noticed that the moment someone near you yawns, you feel an irresistible urge to do the same? Remarkably, even reading the word \"yawn\" or seeing a photo of someone yawning can trigger the reflex. This curious phenomenon has fascinated scientists for decades.\n\nThe leading explanation for contagious yawning involves \"mirror neurons\" and empathy. Mirror neurons are brain cells that fire both when you perform an action and when you observe someone else doing the same thing. Research suggests that yawning spreads through this neural mirroring system. In fact, studies have found that people who score higher on empathy tests are more susceptible to contagious yawning.\n\nInterestingly, contagious yawning isn't unique to humans. Chimpanzees and dogs also catch yawns from others. Dogs, in particular, are more likely to yawn in response to their owner than to a stranger, suggesting that emotional bonding plays a key role.\n\nAs for the purpose of yawning itself, the old theory about oxygen deprivation has largely been replaced. Modern research supports the idea that yawning serves as a brain-cooling mechanism, helping regulate the temperature of the brain. So next time you catch a yawn from someone, take it as a sign that your empathy circuits are working just fine!",
