    content_en:
      "Dip a thin glass tube into water, and you'll notice the water inside the tube rises higher than the surrounding surface. This gravity-defying phenomenon is called capillary action, and it's the very mechanism that allows plants to transport water from their roots to the tips of their leaves.\n\nCapillary action relies on two key forces. The first is adhesion — the tendency of water molecules to cling to other surfaces like glass. The second is cohesion — the attraction water molecules have for one another. In a narrow tube, the ratio of water-to-glass contact increases, so adhesion overpowers cohesion, pulling the water progressively higher.\n\nThis phenomenon shows up everywhere in daily life. Paper towels absorb spills through capillary action. Fountain pen ink travels up the nib the same way. Even tears spread across your eyelids thanks to it. Trees can draw water dozens of meters high because capillary action works hand in hand with transpiration.\n\nThough it may seem simple, capillary action plays a crucial role in modern technology too — from inkjet printers and microchip cooling systems to medical diagnostic test strips. It's remarkable how the teamwork of tiny molecules accomplishes such extraordinary things!",
